---
source: tests/steps/l01_05_lexer_strings/strings.rs
expression: "out.join(\"\\n\")"
---
"\"unclosed"
    [0..9] malformed string: the string is not closed
"\"two\nlines\""
    [0..4] malformed string: a `"` string ends on its line; a string over several lines is written `"""`
"\"\"\"never closed\n"
    [0..16] malformed string: the string is not closed
"\"\\q is not an escape\""
    [1..3] malformed string: no such escape; the escapes are \n \t \r \0 \\ \" \$ \u{…}
"\"\\u{110000}\""
    [1..11] malformed string: this is no Unicode scalar value: a code point is at most 10FFFF and not a surrogate (D800 to DFFF)
"\"\\u{D800}\""
    [1..9] malformed string: this is no Unicode scalar value: a code point is at most 10FFFF and not a surrogate (D800 to DFFF)
"\"\\u0041\""
    [1..3] malformed string: a code point is written `\u{1F600}`
"\"\\u{}\""
    [1..3] malformed string: a code point is written `\u{1F600}`
"\"\"\"\n    in\n  out\n    \"\"\""
    [11..13] malformed string: this line is indented less than the closing `"""`
"\"\"\"\n    in\n\tout\n    \"\"\""
    [11..12] malformed string: this line is indented less than the closing `"""`
"\"ends in a backslash\\"
    [20..21] malformed string: no such escape; the escapes are \n \t \r \0 \\ \" \$ \u{…}
